That means the "standardized digital test report" the PS asks for isn't a template you invent — it's a document that already exists as an international standard. Replicate R76-2's actual section structure and you instantly look more credible than 495 teams who reinvented a generic report layout.

The pass/fail math (MPE tables) — get this exactly right, most teams won't:

Load (in units of e)	Class I	Class II	Class III	Class IIII	MPE (initial verif.)
0 – 50,000e / 5,000e / 500e / 50e					±0.5e
up to 200,000e / 20,000e / 2,000e / 200e					±1.0e
above that					±1.5e

The error of indication must not exceed the maximum permissible error for the applicable class, and for digital indication the error is corrected for the rounding error, and critically — the maximum permissible errors in service are twice the maximum permissible errors fixed for initial verification. Most teams will build one MPE table and forget in-service doubling entirely. Also note: e (verification scale interval) determines the class, and each accuracy class has its own valid range of n (number of scale intervals) — Class I starts at 50,000 intervals with no stated upper bound, Class II runs 100–100,000 intervals, and so on. A correct implementation validates the declared class against the declared e and Max before it even runs the weighing test — a step almost nobody will bother with. 
