The five things that will actually differentiate you

1. A versioned rules engine, not hardcoded if/else
The PS explicitly asks for "supporting future updates whenever OIML recommendations are revised." Store MPE tables, test procedures, and pass/fail thresholds as versioned, data-driven rule sets (JSON/YAML schema evaluated by a small rules engine) rather than baked into code. Every generated report references which R76 edition/rule-version it was evaluated against. This single architectural choice answers a functional requirement almost every other team will skip entirely, and it's genuinely not hard to build in a hackathon window.

2. Tamper-evident, auditable reports
These reports gate legal certification — they need to be defensible, not just pretty PDFs. Implement: a cryptographic hash of the full observation dataset embedded in the report, an append-only audit log of every field edit (who/when/old value/new value), and a QR code linking to a hash-verification endpoint. You don't need real government DSC/e-Sign infrastructure for the demo — a keypair-signed hash embedded as metadata is enough to demonstrate the concept, and you explicitly note eMudra/Aadhaar-eSign as the production integration path.

3. Explainable pass/fail, not a black-box verdict
For every test, show the computed value, the exact MPE formula applied, the OIML clause it came from, and the margin. "PASS — indication error 0.3e against MPE ±0.5e per R76-1 Table 3, Class III, initial verification." This is what an auditor or Controller of Legal Metrology actually needs, and it's a small UI/output decision with outsized credibility impact.

4. OCR-assisted nameplate capture + anomaly flagging
Photograph the instrument's nameplate → auto-extract manufacturer, Max, Min, e, d, serial number via OCR → pre-fill the form. Then run a simple anomaly check across observations (e.g., flag if declared e doesn't produce a valid n for the declared class, or if repeated readings show suspiciously perfect zero-error, which usually means manual fabrication rather than real testing). This is a legitimate, scoped AI feature — not AI-washing — and directly strengthens data-validation, which is a named functional requirement.

5. Narrow and correct beats broad and shallow
Don't try to implement all 12+ R76 test types with real logic in a hackathon window. Pick 3–4 tests and implement them with genuine correctness: Weighing performance (MPE table + in-service doubling), Eccentricity (corner-load formula), Discrimination, and Repeatability. For the rest, show the data-entry screens and mark them "procedure defined, evaluation engine pluggable via rules config" — this is honest, still demonstrates the architecture, and is far more convincing than 12 tests that are all subtly wrong.

Solution architecture

Modules

Instrument & Model Registry — manufacturer, model, Max/Min/e/d, declared class, photos (with the clutter/validity check), linked to a unique model ID
Lab Session Capture — environmental conditions (temp/humidity), test equipment (standard weights with their own calibration traceability), operator, timestamps; offline-first (IndexedDB/local cache + sync) since lab connectivity can't be assumed
Rules Engine — versioned OIML rule sets (MPE tables, class-validity ranges, test procedures) as data, evaluated against captured observations
Report Generator — populates the actual R76-2 section structure, outputs PDF/A (WeasyPrint or similar) and editable DOCX (docxtpl), embeds hash + QR
Audit & Signing — append-only change log, hash chain per report, optional digital signature
Repository & Dashboard — search/filter by model/manufacturer/status/date, instrument-wise history, status pipeline (draft → under review → approved)
Access Control — role-based per the real lab hierarchy (Technician / Reviewing Officer / Approving Authority)

Suggested stack (fast to build, defensible to explain): Next.js/React frontend, FastAPI or Node backend, PostgreSQL, a small Python/JS rules-evaluation layer for the MPE logic, WeasyPrint or Puppeteer for PDF/A generation, docxtpl for Word output.

On the dataset

There's no public dataset of raw NAWI test observations — this is a workflow/compliance tool, not a trained-model problem, so you don't need one in the ML sense. What you do need, and what I'd pull directly:

OIML R76-1 (2006/202x) and R76-2 (2007) PDFs from oiml.org — your MPE tables and report structure come straight from these, not from approximation
Legal Metrology Act, 2009, Legal Metrology (General) Rules, 2011, and Legal Metrology (Approval of Models) Rules, 2011 — cited in the PS itself, and they give you the real role hierarchy and lab-recognition criteria
Synthetic observation data you generate yourself: build instruments across all four classes with deliberately borderline load values (right at the 500e/2,000e/etc. boundaries) so your MPE calculation can be stress-tested live in front of judges — this is far more convincing than a demo that only shows obviously-passing data
For the pitch itself

Since your hackathon format is a 10-minute jury presentation, lead with the two-line insight most teams will miss — "OIML already publishes the exact report format we're asked to generate (R76-2), and India just became the 13th nation authorised to issue OIML certificates — this tool isn't a nice-to-have, it's infrastructure for that mandate" — then do one full live pass: capture → rules-engine evaluation with visible clause citations → signed PDF → repository lookup by QR hash. One correct, explainable, audit-ready flow beats a feature-checklist demo every time.

